\section{Scales, guards and floors}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Random-effect scales.} \code{\link[nlme:VarCorr]{nlme::VarCorr()}} reports the subject SD of \verb{log k}
on the natural-log scale (\code{Term = "k"} means log k); \code{\link[=tidy]{tidy()}} and
\code{\link[=summary]{summary()}} convert it to the log10 scale and label it so. \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}}
returns the standardised deviate \code{u_i} for a \code{k ~ 1} fit but
natural-log offsets (\code{re_k}, \code{re_phi} / \code{re_s}) for a two-random-effect
fit.
\item \strong{Mean guard.} The fitted mean is held inside \verb{[1e-6, 1 - 1e-6]}. For
the exponential equation this binds once \code{k * delay} exceeds about
13.8 (for \code{k = 0.01}, delays beyond roughly 1,400 days); such
observations carry no information about \code{k}, so very steep discounters
measured at long delays lose curvature. Mazur needs \code{k * delay} near
\code{1e6} to reach the guard; Green-Myerson and Rachlin can reach it with a
large \code{s} (e.g. Green-Myerson with \code{k = 0.1}, \code{s = 3} at 1,460 days).
\code{fit$guard_info$mu_guard_lower} / \verb{$mu_guard_upper} count the
positive-delay observations whose subject-level fitted mean falls outside
the guard, and \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}} adds a note when either is non-zero.
\item \strong{Shape \code{s}.} The reported \code{s} is the unclamped population value
\code{exp(log_s)}. With \code{k + s ~ 1} each subject's effective \code{s} is
soft-clamped into \verb{(0.05, 20)}, and the \code{VarCorr()} SD of \code{s} is on the
latent (pre-clamp) log scale. \code{subject_pars} holds both the effective
\code{s} and the latent \code{s_latent = exp(log_s + re_s)};
\code{fit$guard_info$n_s_clamped_lower} / \verb{$n_s_clamped_upper} count the
subjects whose two values differ by more than 1\% on the log scale
(noted by \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}).
\item \strong{Precision floor.} SLT-beta precision is bounded below at
\code{phi = 0.1}. For \code{k ~ 1} this is an optimizer bound that
\code{tmb_control$lower} can relax; with \code{k + phi ~ 1} each subject's \code{phi}
is floored at 0.1 inside the likelihood and cannot be relaxed.
\code{subject_pars$phi_latent} is the unfloored value and
\code{fit$guard_info$n_phi_floor} counts the subjects below the floor
(noted by \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}).
}

These counts are computed at the fitted values and are reporting only:
they do not change any estimate.
}

\section{Two-parameter equations (Green-Myerson, Rachlin)}{

\code{k} and \code{s} trade off along a ridge, and with the few delays of a typical
titration task (about 7) the pair is much less stable than a one-parameter
\code{k}. The pair is particularly sensitive to how responses near zero are
recorded. In the SLT-beta log-density the response enters through a
\code{(mu * phi - 1) * log(y)} term, so a recorded 0 (treated as about \code{1e-8})
carries far more leverage than a recorded 0.001; the direction of the pull
depends on \code{mu * phi}. In simulation with identical true values (Green-
Myerson, \verb{log k = -4.61}, \code{s = 1.4}, 300 subjects, 6 delays), the same
draws gave \verb{(log k, s)} of \verb{(-4.79, 1.58)} as simulated, \verb{(-5.06, 1.91)}
after rounding \code{y} to 3 decimals, and \verb{(-4.37, 1.20)} after flooring \code{y}
at 0.001; Mazur fits to the same data moved by less than 0.03. These are
sensitivity results for that design, not a general bias. Report how the
indifference points were recorded (resolution, how zeros were coded, the
count of \code{y} below 0.001), keep the convention identical across groups
being compared, and consider a sensitivity refit under an alternative
convention.
}

